---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';

const title = 'Planning a meeting between London and Sydney';
const description =
  'How to choose a fair London–Sydney meeting time, account for daylight-saving changes, and communicate the compromise clearly.';
---

<Base title={title} description={description} path="/guides/london-sydney-meeting-times">
  <article class="max-w-2xl">
    <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" class="mb-5 text-xs text-paper-500">
      <a href="/guides" class="ring-focus hover:text-paper-900">Guides</a>
      <span aria-hidden="true" class="mx-1.5">/</span>
      <span aria-current="page">London and Sydney</span>
    </nav>
    <header class="mb-8">
      <p class="text-[11px] font-semibold tracking-[0.15em] text-paper-500 uppercase">
        City-pair guide
      </p>
      <h1 class="mt-2 text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-balance sm:text-4xl">
        Planning a meeting between London and Sydney
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-paper-700">
        London and Sydney are a useful test of whether a meeting plan is fair: the cities are far
        apart, their daylight-saving seasons differ, and there is no hour that is comfortable for
        both people every day of the year.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="mt-8">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">
        Start with the overlap, not the conversion
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-paper-700">
        A conversion tells you the clock reading at one moment. A useful meeting plan starts with
        boundaries: when does the London participant start and finish work, when is Sydney awake,
        and who carries the early-morning or evening cost? For many teams, a London morning means a
        Sydney evening. That can work, but it should be an explicit choice rather than an accidental
        default.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-8">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Why the gap does not stay put</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-paper-700">
        The two cities do not change clocks on the same dates, and their seasons are reversed. The
        difference therefore passes through short transition windows as well as its usual seasonal
        gaps. A weekly meeting that feels fine in one month can move by an hour for the other person
        a few weeks later. Use the <a
          href="/difference/london/sydney"
          class="ring-focus rounded font-medium text-paper-900 underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-paper-700"
          >London–Sydney time-difference page</a
        >
        to see the current gap and the dates it changes.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-8">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Make the compromise visible</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-paper-700">
        Put both local times in the meeting title or description for the first few occurrences, for
        example: “09:00 London / evening Sydney”. If the call repeats, agree whether the London hour
        remains fixed or whether the difficult slot rotates. A quarterly rotation can be kinder than
        asking the same person to take every early or late call.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-8">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">
        Check a scenario before sending the invite
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-paper-700">
        Open the <a
          href="/overlap"
          class="ring-focus rounded font-medium text-paper-900 underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-paper-700"
          >meeting planner</a
        >, add London and Sydney, set realistic working hours and choose a date near the next clock
        change. The planner makes the trade-off visible and produces a shareable link, so the people
        affected can see the same proposal before it becomes a standing meeting.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>
